ROLE: product_realist  VOTE: yellow
REAL_USE_CASE: ML engineers using streaming data loaders (tf.data, WebDataset, MosaicML StreamingDataset) with source/domain-grouped shards (universal real layout) who provision shuffle buffers at 10-50x batch size per practitioner heuristics
OPERATIONAL_SIGNIFICANCE: microbench-only — L0 demonstrates 15.1x gradient-variance inflation at B=10b/L=20b using synthetic gradients (d=32, within-source corr=0.80), but provides NO evidence that this variance inflation translates to measurable convergence degradation (loss spikes, final accuracy) on real model training; packet explicitly marks "NOT metric-validity (real gradient-variance currency)" and reserves downstream validation for L1
ADOPTION_BARRIER: [memory-infeasible B scaling — even B=10L yields 2.17x inflation (still >1.5x), requiring B>>L; real L (full source/shard) can be millions, making B provisioning impossible; alternative "interleave sources/pre-shuffle shards" requires data pipeline changes not just buffer config tuning; no proof that fixing variance improves training outcomes]
RATIONALE: Packet L0 grid shows 15.10x inflation at practitioner B=320 (10b) with L=640 (20b); structural ceiling confirmed: scaling B 2b->10b->50b at L=6400 yields 24.8->23.8->19.1 flat-high; B/L axis collapse verified: B/L 0.1->10.0 reduces inflation 23.3->2.17; BUT packet acknowledges "NOT metric-validity (real gradient-variance currency)" and actionability caveat: "is 'provision B against L' actionable given real L (a full source/shard) can be enormous — or is the real takeaway 'interleave sources / pre-shuffle shards' rather than 'grow B'?" No L1 evidence of downstream impact.
